# Maximum likelihood {#sec-mle}

Building on @def-expectation from @sec-probability, we turn the expectation into a
principle for fitting models. Given data $x_1, \dots, x_n$ drawn independently from a
family $p(\cdot \mid \theta)$, the likelihood is the probability the family assigns to
exactly the data we observed, read as a function of the parameter:
$$ \mathcal{L}(\theta) = \prod_{i=1}^n p(x_i \mid \theta). $$
Maximum likelihood picks the $\theta$ that makes the observed data least surprising.

![The log-likelihood of a Gaussian mean, peaked at the sample average.](likelihood.svg){#fig-likelihood}

@fig-likelihood shows the shape we exploit: a single smooth peak. Two facts make that
peak trustworthy.

::: {.theorem #thm-consistency}
Under mild regularity conditions the maximum-likelihood estimator is **consistent**: as
$n \to \infty$ it converges in probability to the true parameter.
:::

::: {.lemma #lem-score}
The score has zero expectation at the true parameter,
$\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\!\left[\nabla_\theta \log p(X \mid \theta)\right] = 0$.
:::

::: {.proof}
Differentiate the identity $\int p(x \mid \theta)\,\mathrm{d}x = 1$ under the integral
sign. The derivative of the constant is zero, while the left side becomes
$\int \nabla_\theta p = \int p\,\nabla_\theta \log p = \mathbb{E}_\theta[\nabla_\theta \log p]$,
which is therefore zero.
:::

By @thm-consistency the estimator lands in the right place, and @lem-score is the first
step of the standard argument that it does. Compare the broad prior in @fig-distributions
(@sec-probability) with the sharp likelihood of @fig-likelihood here: that concentration
is exactly what consistency promises.
